  "security_implications": "The EDN hold-handshake (G5, A1) ensures that a request dropped due to escalation is not lost, but if edn_ack never arrives, the interface deadlocks, blocking all entropy supply. The priority arbitration (G2-G3, U3) gives clearing PRNG precedence over masking PRNG; a persistent clearing request could starve the masking PRNG, degrading the first-order masking countermeasure and potentially exposing secret key material under side-channel analysis. The synchronized life cycle signal (G6) and the register error outputs (G7) are part of the hardware alert escalation and integrity reporting; incorrect connection or missing alert drive (U4) could hide security faults.",
  "evidence_refs": [
    "aes.sv:7-140"
  ],
